WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:09.000
In 1979 the ancient ruined city of Persepolis
became a UNESCO World Heritage Site.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:15.000
An animation shows how it was likely
to have looked 2500 years ago.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:21.000
This is Persepolis in the 6th century BC.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.000
At the time of the Persian King Darius the First.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.000
It lies at the foot of a mountain on a rocky plateau.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.000
This terrace is part natural
and part constructed, a grandiose creation.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.000
Persepolis served to represent the ruler.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Through the so-called Gate of All Nations.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Once a year, delegations of the
conquered peoples entered the palace district.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:59.000
In a procession, they brought
their gifts to the Persian king.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.000
The Persian architects were great masters of their art.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:12.000
We come to huge halls built on amazingly
narrow columns, spanned by light roofs.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:17.000
The throne hall was reached
via a huge staircase.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.000
That was beautifully decorated.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:28.000
Alexander the Great conquered
the residential city in 330 BC.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.000
And allowed it to be burnt down completely.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:40.000
Traces of the fire can still be seen today
on the partially rebuilt ruins.
